# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-ItalianProgrammedCourse-Text> ,
              <https://it.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://sv.wiktionary.org/> ,
              <https://folkets-lexikon.csc.kth.se/folkets/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Word list. A candidate list of 120 words and phrases for a first Italian course was drawn up by hand from the brief's categories (numbers 0–20, the tens and 100; days; months; colours; family; greetings and polite phrases; everyday nouns; adjectives; verbs), choosing the most basic word for each concept and following the authored French deck where the languages allow. The family has ten members, uncle and aunt included, so that lo (lo zio) appears on a card. No frequency list or textbook word list was copied."@en ,
                 "Wikidata lexemes. SPARQL query 1 fetched every Italian lexeme (language Q652) whose lemma is a candidate (plus buon giorno, buona sera, buona notte, per piacere, scusa, arancio and tavolo, looked at as alternatives), with its category, gender (P5185), senses, English and Italian glosses and sense items (P5137). Four candidates were not found: buonasera, a presto, per favore and scusi. The lexeme search (API calls 2) found no lexeme for buonasera, buona sera, a presto, per favore, per piacere, mi scusi or buon giorno; it found the verb scusare (L1206184), whose form F44 is scusi (third person singular, present imperative). The full JSON of all lexemes found was fetched with API calls 3. For each word the lexeme of the card's part of speech was chosen (numeral for numbers, noun for nouns, days and months, adjective for colours and adjectives, verb for verbs, interjection or adverb for the greetings, sì and no)."@en ,
                 "Wikidata items. The items linked from the chosen senses (P5137), plus four items found with the item search (API calls 5: 17 Q40118, good evening Q19279366, goodbye Q2506912, thank you Q19279214), were fetched with API calls 4 (labels and aliases in it, en, sv and mul). They gave the English and Swedish names of days, months, colours, family members, greetings and everyday things and the digit labels of the numbers. For colours the adjective lexeme gives the front and the feminine form, and the noun lexeme of the same colour links the item (most adjective lexemes have no senses). The labels of the grammatical items used in the lexemes (categories, genders, form features) were fetched with API call 6."@en ,
                 "Phrases from the FSI course. For the phrases with no Wikidata lexeme, the Internet Archive was searched for public-domain Foreign Service Institute Italian courses (call 8). The two searches found the Italian (Programmed) Course, Volume I (1976), with a public-domain licence, one audio lesson of the FSI Italian FAST course, and other items that were not used (lending-library scans titled \"Italian : programmed\" and an ERIC Post Language Program course of unknown status). The OCR text of the Programmed Course was downloaded (call 9) and searched with grep (call 10); passages were then read in context by line range. The course gives buon giorno and buona sera (Recapitulation, Greetings), per favore and per piacere (Unit XXII), arrivederci and arrivederLa (Unit XXII), come sta? (Unit XVII), prego (Unit XXIV), scusi (Unit XXIV), the word for thank you (Unit XVII; written g in the course's abbreviations) and si/no (Unit VIII). It has no ciao, buonanotte or a presto. A presto (see you soon), on the first word list, has neither a Wikidata lexeme nor a public-domain source and was replaced by come sta? (how are you?), which the course teaches. The quotations from the course give its text with OCR errors and spacing normalised (for example \"bsne\" read as bene); where that matters the evidence says so."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the Italian word as in the lexeme's lemma (or the FSI phrase), lower case. Nouns are preceded by the definite article that shows their gender: il and lo (before z, s + consonant, gn and ps) for masculine nouns, la for feminine ones; before a vowel the article is written l' and the gender is added in brackets, m. or f. (l'acqua (f.), l'uomo (m.), l'amico (m.)); the apostrophe is the straight ASCII apostrophe. Days and months are given without an article and in lower case, as Italian writes them, with their gender in a note. Colours and adjectives are given in the masculine singular with the feminine in a note; verbs in the infinitive. Greetings are given in their one-word spelling where Wikidata has that lemma (buongiorno, buonanotte) and, for buonasera, as the FSI course's buona sera written as one word in the same way as Wikidata's lemmas buongiorno (L1165742) and buonanotte (L1165741), a spelling that Italian and English Wiktionary only confirm; the two-word spellings (buon giorno, buona sera, buona notte, as the FSI course writes them) are named in the notes. The back is our own short English and Swedish rendering of the main sense: for nouns the bare noun, for verbs the infinitive (English \"to …\", Swedish \"att …\"), for numbers the digits (tagged zxx, as digits are in no language). English and Swedish follow the Wikidata labels where an item exists and has them; otherwise they are the usual equivalents, chosen by the authors and checked in English Wiktionary, Folkets lexikon and Swedish Wiktionary. Where a word has two everyday equivalents both are given (buongiorno: good morning, good day; ciao: hi, bye; città: city, town; andare: gå, åka; parlare: tala, prata). Rule for text not given by a content source: English and Swedish equivalents and the facts behind the notes come from the authors' own knowledge of the languages, are worded by the authors and are only confirmed in the verification sources; such single words and short facts of usage count as the authors' own content. Per favore is snälla, the usual Swedish word in a spoken request (Folkets lexikon gives it for the interjection please); the Swedish note adds that a spoken Swedish request often ends with tack."@en ,
                 "Notes. A short note was added where it helps a beginner and is confirmed by a source: the gender of each day and month (domenica is the only feminine day); the feminine of each colour and adjective (blu invariable, verde, grande and arancione the same in the feminine; buon before most masculine nouns); the plural, with its article (i, gli, le), of every countable noun, from the lexeme's forms and the Wiktionary head lines (irregular: gli uomini, le mani, le città unchanged; i fratelli also meaning brothers and sisters, i figli children, i nonni grandparents); mano being feminine; lo before z, s + consonant, gn and ps; l'amica; uno also being the article before z, s + consonant, gn and ps, and sei also meaning you are (each with a front note \"number\"); the formal and informal forms (arrivederLa, come stai?, scusa), per piacere, prego to let someone go ahead, non before a verb, and the Swedish gå/åka distinction. Notes are our own wording."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every Italian word was looked up in Italian Wiktionary (calls 12), except come sta?, which has no page there, and no, whose entry gives only marginal senses (English Wiktionary, Wikidata and the FSI course check those two): spelling, part of speech, the gender mark on the head line against the article on the card, the plural, and the first relevant definition (quoted in each card's evidence). Every English meaning was checked against the Italian section of English Wiktionary (calls 13, REST definition API), and for nouns, adjectives, days and months the Italian head template in the page's wikitext (gender, plural, inflection). Every Swedish word was checked in Folkets lexikon (call 15), English → Swedish, or Swedish → English when the first direction had no match, and, where a page with an Italian section exists, in Swedish Wiktionary (calls 14). A Python script built the evidence lines from the responses saved during authoring, so each quote is what the source returned, with wiki markup removed; quotations longer than 160 characters are cut and marked with \"…\". The raw responses and scripts were working files and are not published."@en ,
                 "Dictionaries not used. The brief suggested Treccani or the Accademia della Crusca for checking the Italian. Treccani's terms (https://www.treccani.it/footer/termine-condizioni/, read before any dictionary page) state: \"Ai sensi dell'art. 4 della Direttiva UE 2019/790, si comunica che l'uso di tutti i contenuti presenti sul portale www.treccani.it per attività di text e data mining è espressamente vietato senza preventiva autorizzazione scritta del titolare dei diritti.\" Treccani was therefore not consulted at all; its home page (https://www.treccani.it/) was fetched only to find the link to these terms. The Accademia della Crusca publishes no general dictionary to check words against; only its home page (https://accademiadellacrusca.it/) was fetched, which shows \"© 2019 Accademia della Crusca\" and no licence, and it was not used. Italian Wiktionary and English Wiktionary, whose licences allow automated use, verify every Italian form and gender instead."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. The dossier's Wikidata checks (the Italian, English and Swedish label or alias of each linked item that matches the card's text, and the mul digit label of each number: 251 checks on 98 items) are re-run against live Wikidata by the builder on every build. Wikidata lexemes cannot be checked by the builder (it checks items only); the lexeme facts in the evidence were read from the API responses of 4 October 2026. The built deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (scripts/validate_sources.ts)."@en ,
                 "Excluded: a presto (no open source, see step 5), words with two equally common forms or meanings that a single card cannot separate (il tavolo and la tavola, both table; la strada and la via, both street; azzurro beside blu; marrone and castano for brown), the formal/informal pronouns tu and Lei (which need grammar, not a flashcard; they appear only in notes), the year (anno and annata), and conoscere/sapere (both to know)."@en ,
                 "Selection: 120 cards: the cardinal numbers 0–20, the tens 30–90 and 100 (29); the seven days of the week; the twelve months; eight colours (red, blue, green, yellow, black, white, grey, orange); ten family members (mother, father, brother, sister, son, daughter, grandfather, grandmother, uncle, aunt); ten greetings and polite phrases (buongiorno, buonasera, buonanotte, ciao, arrivederci, come sta?, grazie, per favore, prego, scusi) and yes and no (sì, no); 20 everyday nouns (house, water, bread, milk, apple, book, chair, door, window, dog, cat, city, school, man, woman, friend, hand, day, night, week); six common adjectives (grande, piccolo, buono, cattivo, nuovo, vecchio); and 16 common verbs in the infinitive (essere, avere, andare, fare, venire, parlare, mangiare, bere, amare, volere, potere, vedere, leggere, scrivere, dormire, lavorare). The list was chosen by the deck's authors for a first course, not copied from any frequency list or textbook. Every word has a Wikidata lexeme except buonasera, come sta? and per favore, which come from the public-domain FSI course. Left out: a presto (no open source; come sta? took its place), table and street (two everyday words each: tavolo/tavola, strada/via), brown (marrone, castano) and the light blue azzurro, the pronouns tu and Lei, the pair anno/annata, and conoscere/sapere. Nouns are given with the definite article (il, lo, la); before a vowel the elided l' is followed by the gender, (m.) or (f.); the notes give the plural with its article (i, gli, le) for every countable noun. Colours and adjectives are given in the masculine singular, with the feminine in a note. Days and months are given without an article and in lower case, as Italian writes them; their gender is in a note on every day and month card. Greetings are written as one word (buongiorno, buonasera, buonanotte), with the two-word spelling in the note."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow any reuse: Wikidata's lexemes and items (CC0 1.0: Italian lemmas, genders, forms, glosses and Italian, English and Swedish labels) and the Foreign Service Institute's Italian (Programmed) Course, Volume I (1976), a work of the United States Government with no copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. § 105 (three phrases with their meaning, the facts in four notes, and a check of eight more cards). The selection of words is the authors' own. The English and Swedish renderings not taken from Wikidata are the usual equivalents chosen by the authors; several of them are the same short stock glosses that English Wiktionary, Swedish Wiktionary or Folkets lexikon give, as the source records say. Single words and fixed greetings such as \"good morning\" or \"ingen orsak\" are facts of usage, not protectable expression, so this does not carry those sources' licences into the deck; no definition, example, comment or list was taken from them. The rule applied throughout: English and Swedish equivalents and the facts in the notes not given by a content source are the authors' own knowledge, worded by them and only confirmed in a verification source. Italian Wiktionary, English Wiktionary and Swedish Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) and Folkets lexikon (CC BY-SA 2.5) were used for verification only. Treccani was not used, because its terms forbid text and data mining, and the Accademia della Crusca was not used (method, step 9). So no source requires attribution or share-alike for the deck, although all are credited in its provenance. The quotations in this provenance report are not covered by the deck's CC0 dedication or the repository's MIT licence: quotations from the three Wiktionaries are © their contributors, CC BY-SA 4.0; quotations from Folkets lexikon are CC BY-SA 2.5; quotations from the FSI course are in the public domain; Wikidata data is CC0."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards: every Italian form, gender, plural, English and Swedish meaning and note against the content sources and the verification sources; the builder's Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card has evidence from a content source (Wikidata, or the FSI course for buonasera, come sta? and per favore) and at least two verification sources. Italian Wiktionary agrees with the spelling of every Italian word on the cards except the phrase come sta?, which it has no page for (English Wiktionary does); the gender shown on every noun, day and month card agrees with the Wikidata lexeme (or its forms), Italian Wiktionary and the English Wiktionary head template. English Wiktionary confirms every English back; Folkets lexikon, Swedish Wiktionary or a Swedish Wikidata label confirms every Swedish back. The builder's Wikidata checks cover the Italian, English and Swedish labels or aliases of 97 linked items (249 checks), including the digit label of every number; they all pass. Discrepancies between sources and how they were resolved are listed below. No review round has been run yet."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards, the description and selection; live Wikidata, Folkets lexikon and validate_sources re-run."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No outright factual errors. Two warnings (the Swedish for please; the incomplete rule for uno as an article) and three suggestions (the same gap in the note on lo; home for casa; sì and no counted among the greetings) were verified against the sources and all applied."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards, the title, description and keywords against the built TTL; language tags, Swedish idiom and conventions."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Language tags all correct and no wrong card. Two warnings (the ambiguous Swedish note on no; vänligen for please) and three wording suggestions (come sta?, buonasera, prego) were checked and all applied."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Licences and licence evidence of all sources, the CC0 deck licence, method, queries, licensing paragraph and the evidence of 30 cards spot-checked against live sources."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The CC0 deck licence and every source licence were confirmed. Two warnings (the record of where buonasera's one-word spelling comes from; missing evidence for cattivo's elak) and three suggestions (Folkets' description of uncle; the FSI card count; a garbled English Wiktionary quote) were verified and all applied; the same garbled-quote artefact was found and fixed on buono and fare."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 12 cards changed in rounds 1–3, every third other card (51 cards in all), the deck metadata, the method, the licensing paragraph and the quality-control log, against live Wikidata, English Wiktionary, Folkets lexikon and the FSI course text."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every fix claimed in rounds 1–3 was confirmed in the dossier, and the sampled labels, lexeme facts and language tags matched the live sources. One suggestion, that the newly added meanings home (casa) and make (fare) also be cited from Wikidata, was verified and applied."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Final full-deck review (facts and language)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 120 cards, the deck metadata (title, description, keywords, study direction), the method, the licensing paragraph and the fixes logged in rounds 1–4."@en ,
                 "Outcome: All 120 cards were found correct, with correct language tags and idiomatic Swedish, and every fix from rounds 1–4 was confirmed in the dossier. One optional suggestion, on the Swedish note of per favore, was checked and applied."@en .

